FLOORFORGE_43 — the assistant was quoting a number the site retired, and the
tier prices existed in two hand-kept copies.

THE DEFECT

components/ChatbotPanel.tsx carried this comment above its pricing answer:

    "Kept in step with the pricing section by hand — this string is the one
     place on the site that restates it in prose, so a change there that misses
     here turns the assistant into a second, stale price list."

The warning was correct and the thing it warned about had already happened,
somewhere else in the same file.

FLOORFORGE_31 retired the field-only throughput constant in favour of
`completeFloorSqftPerDay()`, which counts the perimeter the drum physically
cannot reach. Every surface moved to the resulting ~1,400–1,500 sqft band. The
assistant did not. Ask it about the ROI model and it answered:

    "a 1,579 sqft/robot/day throughput target"

A figure that appears nowhere else on the site, roughly 5% high, and high for
precisely the reason the two-machine argument exists — it ignores the perimeter.
The one surface a visitor can interrogate directly was the one surface still
quoting the pre-perimeter number.

Separately, the four software tier prices were typed twice: as JSX literals in
the homepage pricing cards, and inside the assistant's prose. Neither read a
constant. That is the same failure mode as the four different throughput figures
this codebase already had once (audit/PRODUCT_TRUTH.md T0-2).

THE FIX

  lib/product.ts
    * `SOFTWARE_TIERS` — Essentials and Professional, base and per-robot. Values
      lifted from what the site already published, not chosen here. Enterprise
      stays "custom" and is deliberately not a number.
    * `HARDWARE_UNIT_COST_LOW_USD` / `HIGH_USD`, with the existing
      `HARDWARE_UNIT_COST_LABEL` now DERIVED from them. Renders identically
      ("$15–25K") and lets the two-machine capital figure be computed rather
      than typed.
    * `COMPLETE_FLOOR_SQFT_PER_DAY_LABEL` — the published band, computed from
      `completeFloorSqftPerDay()` across typical residential sizes. Change a
      machine's working width in lib/robots.ts and this label moves with it.

  components/ChatbotPanel.tsx
    * Every figure interpolated. Tier prices, RaaS band and term, hardware band,
      machine count, machine NAMES (from lib/robots.ts), grit sequence, labour
      rate, reduction baseline, commercial adjustment. Nothing typed.
    * The two-machine totals are arithmetic on the constants:
      `RAAS_MONTHLY_* x MACHINES_PER_COMPLETE_FLOOR` -> $1,200–2,000/mo, and
      `HARDWARE_UNIT_COST_* x MACHINES_PER_COMPLETE_FLOOR` -> $30–50K.
    * The ROI answer quotes the BAND and says why it is a band: "a perimeter
      grows with the square root of the area while the field grows with the
      area, so the figure moves with room size." Prose with no job size in hand
      must not pick a point from a curve.

  app/page.tsx
    * The two pricing cards read `SOFTWARE_TIERS`. The perimeter note's
      "comparing $299 to $799" became "comparing the two tiers", because that
      sentence was a third copy of the same numbers.

Every disclaimer is untouched and every one still renders: "None of this is
locked or an offer", "no machine has been built and no manufacturer has quoted a
unit cost", "design assumptions, not measured field results", "Essentials covers
the field only". No claim was added. No price changed value — they changed
custody.

